class:: HallDF
summary:: Dragonfly hall reverb UGen
categories:: UGens>Effects
related:: Guides/DragonFlyReverb, Classes/EarlyReflectionsDF, Classes/PlateDF, Classes/RoomDF

Description::

Native SuperCollider port of Michael Willis's Dragonfly Hall Reverb. This UGen combines Dragonfly's Freeverb3 `earlyref` model for early reflections with the `zrev2` late-reverb model to produce lush, dense reverberation suitable for large concert halls. 

The `zrev2` algorithm is a highly modulated, Schroeder-style feedback delay network designed for complex, expansive acoustic spaces. By processing the audio first through geometric early reflection taps and then blooming into a thick, slowly decaying late tail, this UGen accurately emulates the enveloping sound of a massive physical space. It runs the identical C++ DSP code found in the upstream VST3 plugin without requiring a plugin host.

classmethods::

method::ar

argument::inL
Left input.

argument::inR
Right input.

argument::dry
Dry level in percent (`0..100`). Controls the volume of the unaffected input signal.

argument::early
Early-reflection level in percent (`0..100`). Controls the volume of the initial geometric echoes before the tail begins.

argument::late
Late-reverb level in percent (`0..100`). Controls the volume of the dense, blooming reverberation tail.

argument::size
Size in meters (`10..60`). Affects the overall scale of the hall. Larger sizes sound more expansive, taking longer for the reflections to build up and diffuse.

argument::width
Stereo width in percent (`50..150`). Enhances or reduces the panning extremity of the reverb to widen or narrow the stereo image.

argument::predelay
Predelay in milliseconds (`0..100`). The delay before the late reverb starts. Longer predelays separate the source from the reverb tail, increasing clarity and transient punch.

argument::diffuse
Diffusion in percent (`0..100`). Controls how quickly reflections multiply and smear. High diffusion sounds smooth and dense; low diffusion preserves distinct, echo-like delays.

argument::lowCut
Low cut frequency in Hz (`0..200`). Applies a high-pass filter to the tail to prevent low-end mud.

argument::lowXover
Low crossover frequency in Hz (`200..1200`). The frequency below which the `lowMult` decay multiplier is applied.

argument::lowMult
Low-frequency decay multiplier (`0.5..2.5`). Scales the decay time for low frequencies. Values below 1.0 simulate a space where bass dissipates quickly; values above 1.0 simulate resonant, boomy halls.

argument::highCut
High cut frequency in Hz (`1000..16000`). Applies a low-pass filter to simulate high-frequency absorption.

argument::highXover
High crossover frequency in Hz (`1000..16000`). The frequency above which the `highMult` decay multiplier is applied.

argument::highMult
High-frequency decay multiplier (`0.2..1.2`). Scales the decay time for high frequencies. Lower values simulate softer, highly absorbent wall materials (warm halls); higher values simulate hard, reflective surfaces (bright halls).

argument::spin
Modulation spin in Hz (`0..10`). The rate at which the internal delay lines are modulated. Adds chorus-like movement to the tail.

argument::wander
Modulation wander in milliseconds (`0..40`). The depth of the internal delay line modulation. High values make the tail sound lush and thick, but excessive amounts can induce pitch detuning.

argument::decay
Decay time in seconds (`0.1..10`). The time it takes for the mid-band late reverb tail to fade to silence (RT60).

argument::earlySend
Early-to-late send in percent (`0..100`). Controls how much of the early reflections are fed into the late reverb generator, intertwining the initial space cues with the long tail.

argument::modulation
Modulation amount in percent (`0..100`). A macro control scaling the overall intensity of the chorus modulation.

Examples::

code::
(
{
	// Synthetic snare
	var src = WhiteNoise.ar * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.01, 0.2), Impulse.ar(0.5)) * 0.5;
	
	// Synthetic high-bpm hi-hat:
	// var src = HPF.ar(WhiteNoise.ar, 8000) * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.05), Impulse.ar(8)) * 0.5;
	
	// For live input instead:
	// var src = SoundIn.ar(0);
	
	HallDF.ar(
		inL: src,
		inR: src,
		dry: 80.0,
		early: 30.0,
		late: 50.0,
		size: 40.0,
		decay: 2.5
	);
}.play;
)
::
